---
// The privacy notice for hosts: couples, co-hosts and organisers. Guests and
// vendors have their own (`guests.astro`, `vendors.astro`), and `index.astro`
// introduces all three.
import LegalLayout from '../../layouts/LegalLayout.astro'
import PrivacyNav from '../../components/PrivacyNav.astro'
import { LEGAL_ENTITY, draftPending, isPlaceholder } from '@shared/legal'

// Highlight a detail only while THAT detail is still a placeholder. A page-wide
// flag would mark a filled field as pending the moment any other one wasn't.
const ph = (value: string) => (isPlaceholder(value) ? 'placeholder' : undefined)

// Name every field this page publishes that the shared identity check does
// not already cover. Leave one out and the banner can go while that field
// is still a token.
const draft = draftPending(LEGAL_ENTITY.merchantOfRecord, LEGAL_ENTITY.accountDataRetention)
---
<LegalLayout title="Privacy Notice for Hosts">
  {draft && (
    <div class="draft-banner">
      <strong>Draft</strong> — this notice is not final. Some details of the operator are
      still to be confirmed, and this banner disappears once they are.
    </div>
  )}

  <h1>Privacy Notice for Hosts</h1>
  <PrivacyNav current="hosts" />
  <p class="meta">Last updated: 2026-10-30</p>

  <p>
    This notice explains how <span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.name)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.name}</span>, trading as Cire
    (&ldquo;Cire&rdquo;, &ldquo;we&rdquo;), handles
    personal information across the Cire service — this marketing site, the invitation site your
    guests use, the organiser portal, and the vendor portal. Wedding suppliers have their own
    notice, for <a href="/privacy/vendors">vendors</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Two kinds of people use Cire</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Organisers</strong> (couples and their helpers) create an account with us. For your
      account and purchase information, we decide how it is handled, as described here.</li>
    <li><strong>Guests</strong> don&rsquo;t create accounts. Your couple uploads your details to
      manage their wedding, and we process them on the couple&rsquo;s behalf and instructions.
      Three things are ours to decide rather than theirs, and we decide them the same way for
      every wedding: how long guest information survives the event, how the service is secured,
      and what technical telemetry it produces. If you are a guest with questions about why your
      details are in Cire, your couple is the first port of call — but you can always contact us
      directly too.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Organiser accounts</strong> — your name, email address and passkey credentials
      (public key only — no passwords), and the weddings you manage.</li>
    <li><strong>Which organiser changed a guest&rsquo;s reply</strong> — when you record or
      change a guest&rsquo;s RSVP in the organiser portal, we note your account identifier
      against that reply, and each time you change only its status we also keep a dated
      record naming you. This lets a couple with several organisers tell who changed what.</li>
    <li><strong>What your wedding team sees of you</strong> — everyone on a wedding (its
      owners and co-hosts) sees the others&rsquo; musubi name, handle and profile photo, and
      which checklist tasks each of you holds. Your photo is shown only from musubi&rsquo;s own
      servers, and we do not store it.</li>
    <li><strong>Wedding content</strong> — invitation wording, images, event schedules, checklists,
      budgets and seating charts you create.</li>
    <li><strong>Guest information uploaded by organisers</strong> — guest names, household
      groupings, RSVP responses, dietary needs, which table each guest sits at, guests&rsquo;
      email addresses, and similar details the couple chooses to record.</li>
    <li><strong>Your confirmation for a guest&rsquo;s email address</strong> — when you add or
      change a guest&rsquo;s email address, you confirm that the guest agreed to get emails about
      the wedding, and we record your account identifier, the time and the wording you confirmed.
      Because the address did not come from the guest, you also agree to pass our
      <a href="/privacy/guests">guest privacy notice</a> on to them. A guest can see, change or remove an address you added on their
      invitation, and once they remove or replace it, we do not email it about the wedding again,
      even if it is added back. You cannot change an address a guest added themselves, only remove it.</li>
    <li><strong>Vendor enquiries</strong> — the enquiry threads between a couple and the
      vendors they contact. An enquiry sends the couple&rsquo;s wedding name and message to
      the vendor they chose.</li>
    <li><strong>Purchases</strong> — handled by our merchant of record,
      <span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.merchantOfRecord)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.merchantOfRecord}</span>. We receive order confirmation
      (what was bought, for which wedding) but never see your card details.</li>
    <li><strong>Site usage</strong> — the technical logs needed to keep the service secure. We run
      no third-party analytics or advertising trackers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why we collect it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>To provide the service — hosting invitations, collecting RSVPs, powering the planning tools.</li>
    <li>To process purchases and unlock paid add-ons.</li>
    <li>To send transactional email (sign-in codes, security notices) — never marketing without consent.</li>
    <li>To keep the service secure and improve it.</li>
    <li>To keep an accurate record of who changed a guest&rsquo;s reply when several people
      manage one wedding — our legitimate interest in a shared record the couple can rely on.</li>
    <li>To keep a record of who confirmed each guest&rsquo;s agreement to be emailed — our
      legitimate interest, and the couple&rsquo;s, in being able to show the consent each address
      rests on.</li>
    <li>To show the people running a wedding together who else is on it and who is looking
      after each task — our legitimate interest in a team that can see its own members.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>We do not sell personal information, and we do not use it for third-party advertising.</p>

  <h2>Who we share it with</h2>
  <p>We use a small set of service providers to run Cire:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> — hosting, content delivery, databases, and infrastructure
      supporting security features such as rate limiting (global network).</li>
    <li><span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.merchantOfRecord)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.merchantOfRecord}</span> — payment processing, as
      merchant of record for purchases.</li>
    <li><strong>Resend</strong> — transactional email delivery, including vendor claim
      invitations.</li>
    <li><strong>The vendor you enquire with</strong> — an enquiry is sent to that supplier,
      who then holds it under their own responsibility.</li>
    <li><strong>Grafana Cloud</strong> — service telemetry (metrics and traces; pseudonymised
      identifiers only, never guest content).</li>
    <li><strong>Google Fonts</strong> — our pages currently load fonts from Google&rsquo;s CDN,
      which sees your IP address when they load. We plan to self-host these fonts.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We also disclose information where the law requires it. Our primary region is
    <strong>Oceania</strong>: our databases, which hold your sign-in sessions too, are hosted by
    Cloudflare in its Oceania region, and we operate from Australia. Cloudflare keeps read-only
    copies of those databases in other regions of its network, which it chooses and can change.
    Files &mdash; the images and spreadsheets you upload, and the copies we keep so a change can be
    undone &mdash; are stored by Cloudflare in Eastern North America, and the images are also
    cached in the Cloudflare data centres where they are viewed. The portal&rsquo;s live updates
    run on a Cloudflare server placed near the first request for each wedding, which holds only
    who is listening, and only while they are. Cloudflare counts rate limits wherever in its
    network a request arrives. Some technical records &mdash; email delivery, service telemetry,
    payment processing &mdash; are handled by providers outside Oceania. If you are in the EEA or
    the UK, that means your information is transferred out of that area to Oceania and elsewhere;
    ask us and we will tell you what protections apply to that transfer.
  </p>

  <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
  <p>
    Only as long as needed for the purposes above. Guest information (guest lists, RSVPs, dietary
    needs, email addresses and the record of who added them) is automatically deleted <strong>one year after the wedding&rsquo;s final event</strong>,
    now and then up to 30 days later while the couple&rsquo;s record of their gifts is written.
    That window is set by us and applies to every wedding — an organiser cannot extend it. When it
    is deleted, the couple keep a record of their gifts with no guest names in it — how many were
    reserved and bought from their list, and the amount, currency and day of each money gift —
    until they delete their wedding.
    Other wedding and account data is kept while your account is active and removed when you ask us
    to. The dated record of a status change naming the organiser who made it is deleted after
    <strong>90 days</strong>; the note of who last changed a reply goes with the reply. Specific
    periods for account data:
    <span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.accountDataRetention)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.accountDataRetention}</span>.
  </p>

  <h2>Security</h2>
  <p>
    Traffic is encrypted in transit, organiser sign-in uses passkeys rather than passwords, and
    session credentials are stored hashed. No system is perfectly secure, but we design for
    security first.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You may have rights to access, correct, delete or port your information, and to object to
    certain uses — under the Australian Privacy Act, and for people in the EEA or UK, the GDPR and
    UK GDPR. To exercise them, contact us at
    <span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail}</span>. You can also complain to a regulator —
    in Australia, the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner (OAIC), or your local data
    protection authority.
  </p>

  <h2>Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    We use only the cookies the service needs to work (such as session cookies for signed-in
    organisers and guests). No advertising or cross-site tracking cookies.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    <span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.name)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.name}</span> (trading as Cire),
    <span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.postalAddress)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.postalAddress}</span> —
    <span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail}</span>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
